# the changes game mutants lets by, one per line as NAME :: WHY, the
# name as the report prints it. a change belongs here only when it
# makes no difference to anything the package promises; a change a
# test could tell apart gets the test instead.
cds/cds.go package: shortest = -1 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back => shortest = -2 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back :: strconv.FormatFloat takes any precision below zero as the fewest digits that read back, so -2 writes every number as -1 does
irsa/irsa.go package: shortest = -1 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back => shortest = -2 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back :: strconv.FormatFloat takes any precision below zero as the fewest digits that read back, so -2 writes every number as -1 does
tap/tap.go package: shortest = -1 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back => shortest = -2 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back :: strconv.FormatFloat takes any precision below zero as the fewest digits that read back, so -2 writes every number as -1 does
votable/votable.go package: shortest = -1 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back => shortest = -2 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back :: strconv.FormatFloat takes any precision below zero as the fewest digits that read back, so -2 writes every number as -1 does
cmd/celestial-data/verbs.go package: bits = 64 => bits = 65 :: strconv.ParseFloat reads at 32 bits only when told 32, and at 64 for any other size, so 65 reads every number as 64 does
listing.go package: every = -1 // defined by regexp: no limit on the matches found => every = -2 // defined by regexp: no limit on the matches found :: regexp's FindAll calls take any count below zero as no limit, so -2 finds every link as -1 does
sky.go apart: return onceRound(to-from+halfTurn) - halfTurn => return onceRound(to-from-halfTurn) - halfTurn :: adding half a turn and taking it away differ by one whole turn, which onceRound takes away, so the difference is the same short way round
sky.go Reaches: nearest >= northPole => nearest > northPole :: at a circle that reaches the pole exactly, the cosine of 90 degrees in float64 is 6e-17, not none, so the radius over it is some 1e16 degrees and every longitude is reached either way
sky.go Reaches: return across <= patch.halfLon+circle.radius/shrink => return across < patch.halfLon+circle.radius/shrink :: the two differ only where the distance east equals the reach to the last bit. the reach is a quotient by a cosine, and the distance passes through a sum with 180 degrees that rounds it, so no circle can be placed there: the edge is a point no input lands on
usgs/usgs.go package: bits = 64 => bits = 65 :: strconv.ParseFloat reads at 32 bits only when told 32, and at 64 for any other size, so 65 reads every number as 64 does
naturalearth/naturalearth.go package: upward = 1 // defined: positive infinity => upward = 2 // defined: positive infinity :: math.Inf takes only the sign of its argument, positive infinity for zero or more, so 2 starts the box's west and south at positive infinity as 1 does
naturalearth/naturalearth.go package: downward = -1 // defined: negative infinity => downward = -2 // defined: negative infinity :: math.Inf takes only the sign of its argument, negative infinity for any below zero, so -2 starts the box's east and north at negative infinity as -1 does
naturalearth/naturalearth.go outside: return lon < bounds.west || lon >= bounds.east || lat < bounds.south || lat > bounds.north => return lon < bounds.west || lon >= bounds.east || lat < bounds.south || lat >= bounds.north :: a place at the box's north latitude has no corner above it, so no edge has one end above the place and one not, no edge crosses its ray, and the ray test says sea as the box does. in a run over 20000 random rings and 1.5 million places, none at or above the north was held
naturalearth/naturalearth.go crosses: (lon < at) => (!(lon < at)) :: the ring is closed, so an even number of its edges cross the place's latitude, and the crossings east of the place and those west of it or on it are both odd or both even: counting the west ones gives the same parity. in a run over 20000 random rings and 1.5 million places, no place was held by one count and not by the other
naturalearth/naturalearth.go crosses: lon < at => lon >= at :: the ring is closed, so an even number of its edges cross the place's latitude, and the crossings east of the place and those west of it or on it are both odd or both even: counting the west ones gives the same parity. in a run over 20000 random rings and 1.5 million places, no place was held by one count and not by the other
arxiv/papers.go package: unpaced time.Duration = 0 => unpaced time.Duration = 1 :: the library makes no limiter at zero and one of a nanosecond at one; a nanosecond's limiter holds back no request, since the gate starts each at least three seconds after the last, and a request whose context has ended never reaches the library, since the gate refuses it first. every request goes through alike
arxiv/papers.go answered: !ok || id.version > before.id.version => !ok || id.version >= before.id.version :: the two differ only for two entries of one version of one paper, which arXiv does not answer: it gives one entry a version. were it to, both would be the same paper, named for the same file and fetched from the same address, so keeping the first or the last fetches the same thing
gaia/catalogue.go package: noInteger = 0 // defined: the whole number given after an error => noInteger = 1 // defined: the whole number given after an error :: whole gives noInteger only after an error, and Source returns on the error without reading it
gaia/catalogue.go whole: if read.err != nil { return noInteger } => if read.err != nil { } :: after a failed read of a row every later read of it fails the same way, since the rows are read whole from the same file; the early return only spares the read
gaia/catalogue.go floats: if column(which) == sourceID || column(which) == paramsSolved || read.err != nil { continue } => if column(which) == sourceID || column(which) == paramsSolved || read.err != nil { } :: the two whole-number columns read through Float store a rounded id and the parameters at places nothing reads, since Source takes both through whole; and after an error the reads fail the same way as above
gaia/catalogue.go floats: column(which) == sourceID || column(which) == paramsSolved || read.err != nil => column(which) == sourceID && column(which) == paramsSolved || read.err != nil :: no column is both, so neither is skipped, and Float stores a rounded id and the parameters at places nothing reads, since Source takes both through whole
gaia/catalogue.go floats: column(which) == sourceID || column(which) == paramsSolved || read.err != nil => (column(which) == sourceID || column(which) == paramsSolved) && read.err != nil :: skipping only a whole-number column after an error reads the rest the same: Float stores a rounded id and the parameters at places nothing reads, since Source takes both through whole, and after a failed read of a row every later read of it fails the same way
sdssv/sdssv.go package: anyFields = -1 // defined by encoding/csv: rows of any length => anyFields = -2 // defined by encoding/csv: rows of any length :: encoding/csv takes any FieldsPerRecord below zero as rows of any length, so -2 reads as -1 does
sdssv/sdssv.go package: shortest = -1 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back => shortest = -2 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back :: strconv.FormatFloat takes any precision below zero as the fewest digits that read back, so -2 writes every number as -1 does
internal/caom/caom.go package: shortest = -1 // defined: strconv's precision for the fewest digits => shortest = -2 // defined: strconv's precision for the fewest digits :: strconv.FormatFloat takes any negative precision as the shortest, shortest := prec < 0 in Go 1.26's internal/strconv/ftoa.go, so -2 writes every number as -1 does
spherex/spherex.go package: shortest = -1 => shortest = -2 :: strconv.FormatFloat takes any precision below zero as the fewest digits that read back, so -2 writes every number as -1 does
spherex/whole.go unitsOf: at = max(next, at+blockSize) => at = max(next, at-blockSize) :: a unit that was read ends at least a block after it began, since its header is a block at least, so next is over at+blockSize and over at-blockSize, and max gives next either way. the max is there so that a walk can never stand still
spherex/spherex.go package: noNanos = 0 => noNanos = 1 :: a moment is written to the minute, and one nanosecond more never takes a moment over a minute's end, since the seconds it is made from are whole
cmd/celestial-data/ads.go package: noNanoseconds = 0 // defined by time.Unix: a whole second => noNanoseconds = 1 // defined by time.Unix: a whole second :: until writes the reset in RFC 3339, which holds whole seconds and no fraction, so a nanosecond past the second writes the same time
ads/ads.go heard: len(kept) > mostHeard => len(kept) >= mostHeard :: what is exactly mostHeard long is cut to mostHeard, which is all of it, so it is said the same
ads/ads.go package: dropped = -1 => dropped = -2 :: strings.Map leaves out a rune for any negative mark, so -2 leaves out every rune -1 does
ads/session.go package: onePast = 1 => onePast = 2 :: a token's file is refused when it holds more than mostToken bytes; reading two past instead of one refuses every such file the same, and reads no file differently that holds mostToken or fewer
ads/session.go Load: file, err := os.OpenFile(path, os.O_RDONLY|syscall.O_NOFOLLOW|syscall.O_NONBLOCK, 0) => file, err := os.OpenFile(path, os.O_RDONLY|syscall.O_NOFOLLOW|syscall.O_NONBLOCK, 1) :: OpenFile uses its mode only to make a file, with O_CREATE, which Load never asks, so any mode opens the same
bsc5/catalogue.go package: bits64 = 64 => bits64 = 65 :: strconv.ParseFloat reads at 32 bits only when told 32, and at 64 for any other size, so 65 reads every number as 64 does
bsc5/catalogue.go rowOf: short := max(record-len(line), none) => short := max(record+len(line), none) :: a row padded past a record holds only spaces past it, and every field read ends inside the record, so each field reads the same
dasch/asker.go package: onePast = 1 => onePast = 2 :: an answer is refused when it holds more than mostBytes; reading two past instead of one refuses every such answer the same, and reads no answer differently that holds mostBytes or fewer
dasch/asker.go wait: if asking.last.IsZero() { return nil } => if asking.last.IsZero() { } :: with no question asked yet, the pace since the zero time ended long before now, so the wait that follows sleeps for nothing and returns nil the same
dasch/asker.go package: longestSeconds = int(^uint64(0)>>1) / int(time.Second) => longestSeconds = int(^uint64(1)>>1) / int(time.Second) :: ^uint64(1) differs from ^uint64(0) only in its lowest bit, which the shift right by one drops, so both are the same number
dasch/dasch.go Find: len(sources) > archive.most => len(sources) >= archive.most :: sources exactly archive.most many are cut to archive.most, which is all of them, so the same sources are named
gaia/gaia.go package: shortest = -1 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back => shortest = -2 // defined by strconv: the fewest digits that read back :: strconv.FormatFloat takes any negative precision as the fewest digits that read back, so -2 writes every number as -1 does
